  "reason": "The records collectively make the later study plausibly reconstructible. R2 already combines nanoindentation and elevated-temperature tribology in an HEA film, while R3 and R20 establish temperature- or microstructure-dependent HEA wear behavior. R4 and R13 provide temperature-controlled molecular-dynamics deformation studies of compositionally similar FCC HEAs and connect elevated temperature to HCP evolution, dislocation processes, softening, and stress fluctuations. R16 independently links temperature to FCC/HCP stability and stacking-fault energetics, and R1, R7, R8, and R15 reinforce temperature-dependent dislocation-mediated mechanical behavior. An expert could therefore plausibly combine elevated-temperature coating tribology with atomistic indentation/friction simulation and analyze dislocations, stacking faults, HCP atoms, disorder, hardness, and modulus. The exact FeNiCrCoCu-on-Cu geometry, threshold near 900 K, reduction in dislocation density, and increasing friction are not individually disclosed, and some experimental wear literature reports the opposite friction trend, but these are specific implementations and outcomes rather than a wholly new intervention or mechan"
